% lomo_calendar.le — the obligations of a LOMO description through time,
% in Logical English for LPS.
%
% The timeless reading of a LOMO description is LE2's library lomo.le: who
% is bound, by which date, as of a given day. This library is the reading
% through time: a duty starts when its trigger happens to a party, ends when
% the party does the action in time, and is breached when the clock passes
% its due time. A repeating duty ("every year, three times") starts its next
% occurrence when the clock passes the due time of the current one. A run
% shows, cycle by cycle, which duties are open, met and breached: the
% "deadline calendar" of LOMO's requirements document, running.
%
% A program of a case extends this library. The LOMO translator of LE2
% writes such programs (migration/lomo/lomo_lps.pl): the facts of who is
% bound come from the timeless reading of the register and the case, and
% each date or moment of the case is a number, a *time*: a number of days
% when the case gives dates, a number of minutes when it gives moments
% ("2026-03-01T14:30"), because LE for LPS counts, it does not do calendar
% arithmetic. The first lines of each program say which. What follows a
% breach is not in LOMO; a program can add it as one more law.
%
% Copyright Miguel Calejo; Apache License 2.0.

the target language is: lps.

the events are:
    *an event* happens to *a party* at time *a time*; known as lomo_happens.
    *a party* performs *an action* at time *a time*; known as lomo_performs.
    the clock reaches time *a time*; known as lomo_clock.

the fluents are:
    *a party* owes *an action* under *a rule* by time *a due time*; known as lomo_owes.
    *a party* met *an action* under *a rule* by time *a due time*; known as lomo_met.
    *a party* breached *an action* under *a rule* by time *a due time*; known as lomo_breached.
    the rhythm of *a rule* for *a party* is at occurrence *a number*; known as lomo_rhythm_at.

the templates are:
    *a rule* binds *a party* to *an action* after *an event* within *a duration*.
    the period *a duration* after time *a time* is time *a later time*.
    *a rule* binds *a party* to *an action* every *a duration*.
    the occurrence *a number* of *a rule* for *a party* is due by time *a time*.

the knowledge base lomo calendar includes:

% A trigger happens: the duty starts, with its due time.
when an event happens to a party at time a time
    and a rule binds the party to an action after the event within a duration
    and the period the duration after time the time is time a due time
then the party owes the action under the rule by time the due time.

% The action, in time: the duty is met and ends.
when a party performs an action at time a time
    and the party owes the action under a rule by time a due time
    and the time <= the due time
then the party met the action under the rule by time the due time
    and it is not the case that the party owes the action under the rule by time the due time.

% The clock passes the due time: the duty is breached and ends.
when the clock reaches time a time
    and a party owes an action under a rule by time a due time
    and the time > the due time
then the party breached the action under the rule by time the due time
    and it is not the case that the party owes the action under the rule by time the due time.

% A repeating duty: when the clock passes the due time of its current
% occurrence, met or not, the next occurrence starts (if there is one).
when the clock reaches time a time
    and the rhythm of a rule for a party is at occurrence a number K
    and the occurrence K of the rule for the party is due by time a due time
    and the time > the due time
    and a number K1 = K + 1
    and the occurrence K1 of the rule for the party is due by time a next due time
    and the rule binds the party to an action every a duration
then the rhythm of the rule for the party is at occurrence K1
    and it is not the case that the rhythm of the rule for the party is at occurrence K
    and the party owes the action under the rule by time the next due time.
